From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 17:00:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] pinctrl: sunxi: h713: the IRQ function is mux 0xE

No GPIO interrupt on the H713 could ever fire. Both pin tables give every
pin's IRQ function muxval 0x6, and patch 0004 then skips the switch into the
IRQ function altogether whenever irq_read_needs_mux is false, on the theory
that "IRQ works in GPIO mode, mux to func6 kills input". Both halves are
wrong for this SoC.

The stock 5.4 kernel (DWARF symbols) describes both controllers as
SUNXI_PCTL_HW_TYPE_1 -- the layout these drivers already select with
SUNXI_PINCTRL_NEW_REG_LAYOUT -- whose irq_mux_val is 0xE, and its pin
tables list the "irq" function at muxval 14 for all 132 main-PIO pins and
all 21 R_PIO pins. Its irq_read_needs_mux = false means what it means
upstream: the data register still reads the pin while it is muxed to IRQ.
It is not a reason to leave the pin in gpio_in.

Measured on the bench board on 2026-10-07 with the case power button on
PL4 (gpio-keys, double-edge EINT enabled): with the pin left in gpio_in, as
0004 leaves it, three presses moved the data register 6 times and raised 0
interrupts. With PL4 switched to 0xE the first press raised KEY_POWER at
once (and logind powered the board off), and the data register still read
the idle level. 0x6 is not a working value either: 0x6 and 0x0 behave the
same.

Restore the unconditional mux switch and use 0xE in both tables. The
alternate functions in the R_PIO comment block are inherited and also
disagree with stock in places (stock: PL4 = s_twi1 fn2, s_jtag0 fn3); they
are left for a separate audit of the tables.

Signed-off-by: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/pinctrl/sunxi/pinctrl-sunxi.c
+++ b/drivers/pinctrl/sunxi/pinctrl-sunxi.c
@@ -1054,13 +1054,8 @@
 		return ret;
 	}
 
-	/* Change muxing to INT mode.
-	 * Some SoCs (e.g. H713) can detect edges in GPIO mode and setting
-	 * the IRQ mux function actually disconnects the input path.
-	 * Skip mux change when irq_read_needs_mux is false.
-	 */
-	if (pctl->desc->irq_read_needs_mux)
-		sunxi_pmx_set(pctl->pctl_dev, pctl->irq_array[d->hwirq], func->muxval);
+	/* Change muxing to INT mode */
+	sunxi_pmx_set(pctl->pctl_dev, pctl->irq_array[d->hwirq], func->muxval);
 
 	return 0;
 }
